{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/geist-the-sin-eaters/35780-how-goes-your-boneyarding","id":35780,"title":"How goes your Boneyarding?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Geist: The Sin-Eaters"],"posts":[{"date":"2013-12-23T13:39:22-08:00","id":35780,"title":"How goes your Boneyarding?","content":"In my eyes, Boneyard is one of the most unique and truly awesome abilities in Geist. I know I'm partial to it mainly because I happen to like to gather information about...well...everything. (And again, in my eyes, Boneyard is one of the premiere powers capable of doing so, with respects to Oracles &quot;instant-intuitive-answer&quot; ability.) That all being said, I've never actually ran a game, nor ran in a game where a PC used Boneyard. I will (hopefully) soon be participating in a game where I've chosen it as one of my primary powers, and I've already spoken to my ST about how he looks to be handling it. When I ST'd in along side another fellow in a multi-genre game, i was the head of Sin Eaters, and had decided my own interpretations of the power. I'm curious how others have decided to follow it. Below is my ideas I used for the various parts that were a little questionable to me; I look forward to further input.<br /> <br /> 1. &quot;While the Boneyard is unlocked, the Sin-Eater can generally sense any particular person or thing which she wishes to locate within the affected area without a roll, provided that the object of her search is not actively hiding from her (either magically or through mundane means). She can project her senses to any spot within the Boneyard and examine the area as if she were standing there.&quot; --**The first part of this statement was changed just a little in my game, for future players. I amended that yes, while they could easily locate someone they knew was in their Boneyard (so long as they weren't actively hiding from them) that depending on the size of it, they could also sort of sense vague things as well, such as: &quot;You can sense a large collection of people in the northern portion of your Boneyard.&quot; or &quot;There are several 'lesser' creatures within this forest, but they appear to be rapidly vacating the area.&quot; If they wanted to get a better look at something, I insisted they &quot;manifest their senses&quot; in a location and look, which moves us on to the second portion of this statement. When specifically/actively searching an area with their senses projected, I elected to do two things: first, give a general description of the area they found themselves in, (giving a few extra tidbits for every 4 dice they had in their dice pool: nothing earth-shattering, or specific: but more like...baiting them to look closer: heh.) and then asked for a &quot;search/perception&quot; roll, if they were doing more than giving the area a rough-glance. (Just like you might if they were physically there; as the power more or less lists) Considering you can project your senses with just a -thought-, I figured that being able to rapidly check out their Boneyard was both in order, but could easily be abused. Therefor, I elected to let them have either 3 &quot;rough looks/descriptions&quot; of a place they projected, 2 perception rolls for specific searching in areas they found themselves in, or One action that was basically them projecting in one place, and then searching in a -path-, or moving in a direction without &quot;jumping&quot; to another portion of their Boneyard. Something of a &quot;tracking perception&quot; roll, or &quot;perception in a line&quot;, type-dealy. Again, I wanted them to have the omnipresence that comes with Boneyard, but the reality that it still took a reasonable amount of time to both look hither and yond; and process what your character was seeing.<br /> <br /> 2. &quot;While the Boneyard is unlocked, the Sin-Eater\u2019s body must remain in a trance-like state.&quot; --**I had a hard time with this one; the geek in me reared it's ugly head, and was like: &quot;MORE SPECIFICS!&quot; Meh. In the cross-genre game, I finally elected to say that anyone entering the Boneyard had to be more or less stationary of their own accord; I.E. laying down, and more or less motionless. Not that they couldn't itch their nose, burp, or the like, but....just that it took a great deal of concentration to have your senses this far expanded, and I mean....how could you walk like this? *laughs merrily* We did have a running joke in my game, that a pair of Sin Eaters could work in tandem, one &quot;conscience&quot; and pulling the other in a little Radio Flyer. (Red wagon) I could see how one could argue that it's just a &quot;trance-like&quot; state, and that different states of trance can be ambulatory. I'm open to suggestions on this.<br /> <br /> 3. When one unlocks the Boneyard, according to your Dot/Level rating, you get so many yards/tens of yards/hundreds of yards/miles of Boneyard-space. --**Because of a few hints in Industrial and Coldwind, I had assumed that this power unlocks in more or less a Sphere radius; meaning that it also affects targets up in the air, and down below. (Also attributing Grave-dirt's talk of catacombs and tunnel-systems) I had generally accepted that one could affect one's entire environment in this way, being that you could project your senses up and above different areas to get a wider view of how things are laid out, or if I want to catch something farther than 10' above my &quot;head&quot;. I could also see the argument where it states that your plasm and very being dip down into the ground, and it's called a &quot;Boneyard&quot; not a &quot;BoneAirSpace&quot;, in the case of messing with airplanes, birds, and flying rocks. This would translate to me that I could only &quot;reach&quot; as high as perhaps the tallest &quot;target&quot; so long as it was touching the ground.<br /> <br /> *nods and gestures to the general crowd*<br /> <br /> I'm eager to hear your thoughts.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1219-shake","name":"Shake","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1219_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"20"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-12-23T16:46:01-08:00","id":35910,"title":"","content":"I have two characters (Natalie Knight and Simon Fishborne) in my current <i>Balance of Shadows</i> game (almost 100 sessions) with maxed Boneyard almost from the beginning. Also my previous 5 session mini chronicle <i>Brand New Key</i> had two high Boneyard characters, and I ran a one shot crossover game with 3 Sin-Eaters, a Werewolf, and a Promethean, <i>Heavy Rain (and Zombies)</i>, with one maxed Boneyard user. Here are my POVs based on 103 sessions of it.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Shake</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/geist-the-sin-eaters/35780-how-goes-your-boneyarding#post35780\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">1. &quot;While the Boneyard is unlocked, the Sin-Eater can generally sense any particular person or thing which she wishes to locate within the affected area without a roll, provided that the object of her search is not actively hiding from her (either magically or through mundane means). She can project her senses to any spot within the Boneyard and examine the area as if she were standing there.&quot; --**The first part of this statement was changed just a little in my game, for future players. I amended that yes, while they could easily locate someone they knew was in their Boneyard (so long as they weren't actively hiding from them) that depending on the size of it, they could also sort of sense vague things as well, such as: &quot;You can sense a large collection of people in the northern portion of your Boneyard.&quot; or &quot;There are several 'lesser' creatures within this forest, but they appear to be rapidly vacating the area.&quot; If they wanted to get a better look at something, I insisted they &quot;manifest their senses&quot; in a location and look, which moves us on to the second portion of this statement. When specifically/actively searching an area with their senses projected, I elected to do two things: first, give a general description of the area they found themselves in, (giving a few extra tidbits for every 4 dice they had in their dice pool: nothing earth-shattering, or specific: but more like...baiting them to look closer: heh.) and then asked for a &quot;search/perception&quot; roll, if they were doing more than giving the area a rough-glance. (Just like you might if they were physically there; as the power more or less lists) Considering you can project your senses with just a -thought-, I figured that being able to rapidly check out their Boneyard was both in order, but could easily be abused. Therefor, I elected to let them have either 3 &quot;rough looks/descriptions&quot; of a place they projected, 2 perception rolls for specific searching in areas they found themselves in, or One action that was basically them projecting in one place, and then searching in a -path-, or moving in a direction without &quot;jumping&quot; to another portion of their Boneyard. Something of a &quot;tracking perception&quot; roll, or &quot;perception in a line&quot;, type-dealy. Again, I wanted them to have the omnipresence that comes with Boneyard, but the reality that it still took a reasonable amount of time to both look hither and yond; and process what your character was seeing.</div> </div> </div> </div>I also have them able to examine specific spots, not as much the boneyard at large. They can &quot;run around&quot; in the boneyard to sweep it quickly, or &quot;jump&quot; locations, but in general I require them to look in one area at a time (not that it takes an activation or die roll to switch). I require Perception rolls to notice things as per. I also stress none of their sin-eater senses work, well outside Stigma that allows some, in the boneyard...they may normally realize someone is a vampire on sight because they are dead, but seeing a vampire in the boneyard they would not automatically know (unless they knew the vamp, of course)<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> 2. &quot;While the Boneyard is unlocked, the Sin-Eater\u2019s body must remain in a trance-like state.&quot; --**I had a hard time with this one; the geek in me reared it's ugly head, and was like: &quot;MORE SPECIFICS!&quot; Meh. In the cross-genre game, I finally elected to say that anyone entering the Boneyard had to be more or less stationary of their own accord; I.E. laying down, and more or less motionless. Not that they couldn't itch their nose, burp, or the like, but....just that it took a great deal of concentration to have your senses this far expanded, and I mean....how could you walk like this? *laughs merrily* We did have a running joke in my game, that a pair of Sin Eaters could work in tandem, one &quot;conscience&quot; and pulling the other in a little Radio Flyer. (Red wagon) I could see how one could argue that it's just a &quot;trance-like&quot; state, and that different states of trance can be ambulatory. I'm open to suggestions on this. </div> </div> </div>Same, pretty much. Carrying the character is an option, but past that they can't really move. They can speak but they sound distracted and to someone who does not know what they are doing sleepy, drunk, or high.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> 3. When one unlocks the Boneyard, according to your Dot/Level rating, you get so many yards/tens of yards/hundreds of yards/miles of Boneyard-space. --**Because of a few hints in Industrial and Coldwind, I had assumed that this power unlocks in more or less a Sphere radius; meaning that it also affects targets up in the air, and down below. (Also attributing Grave-dirt's talk of catacombs and tunnel-systems) I had generally accepted that one could affect one's entire environment in this way, being that you could project your senses up and above different areas to get a wider view of how things are laid out, or if I want to catch something farther than 10' above my &quot;head&quot;. I could also see the argument where it states that your plasm and very being dip down into the ground, and it's called a &quot;Boneyard&quot; not a &quot;BoneAirSpace&quot;, in the case of messing with airplanes, birds, and flying rocks. This would translate to me that I could only &quot;reach&quot; as high as perhaps the tallest &quot;target&quot; so long as it was touching the ground.<br /> <br /> *nods and gestures to the general crowd*<br /> <br /> I'm eager to hear your thoughts. </div> </div> </div>I allow somewhat areal perception, but it is more useful for spotting movement then anything else. I may allow a Perception roll to see someone they are looking for, depending (easier day then night, etc). Trees are not their friend. Basically it's usefulness varies. It is character focused; if used on an airplane it would be centered on the character on the plane, not hundreds of miles below.<br /> <br /> The biggest HR I have with Boneyard is I do not allow any other Manifestations to be used through it. It can circumvent the horror too much if they can Boneyard an area and object read everything in it, for example, or curse everyone or animate all the cadavers. More importantly, it limits somewhat their ability to kill at a range. Some boneyards still have attacks but they tend to be very expensive. It's not proven a problem. It's removed a lot of headaches to use this rule.<br /> <br /> I also allow some critters (Noteably Vampires with Auspex) to have a chance to feel the presence of the character watching them. They cannot do anything about it, or know who it is or where they are, but they can sense it. other critters may require their power be turned on, or something. A member of the Colony in Werecat form may can sense someone is around but, again, not who, for instance.<br /> <br /> Mages are the big exception. If they possess the right Arcana, they can sense it (if they look or happen to have such a power active) analyze it and even use it as a sympathetic link if in the boneyard to attack the Sin-Eater using the power. This requires very specific Arcana but is theoretically possible. Bone Dry, the current Mage antagonist in <i>Balance of Shadows</i>, can do this. The previous Mage antagonist Louisa could not.<br /> <br /> Boneyard is an awesome power but can take away from the horror or mystery of a setting. It is a key balance to balance that with the awesomeness of being able to haunt an area and scout or search it. It may be my favorite Geist power.<br /> <br /> I should note the other main New WoD GM here (who plays in <i>Balance of Shadows</i>) does not like Boneyard at all. They deal with it in BoS, but none of the Sin-Eaters that showed up in their Changeling game had it, and they have said if they ever ran Geist they would block it. They worry about the power level, but the biggest thing is the ability to &quot;safely&quot; explore an area not risk investigation. They could search a house, even seeing any ghosts with the right power, without entering, so the other GM does not like it at all. Obviously, I disagree, but there is anouther perspective.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/OnyxGraphics/OnyxMod.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Onyx Path Moderator </span><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/onyx-path-announcements/206-forum-rules\" target=\"_blank\">Forum Rules</a><br /> <span style=\"color:#FF0000\">This is my mod voice.</span> This is my goth voice.<br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/geist-the-sin-eaters/15731-geist-balance-of-shadows-actual-play\" target=\"_blank\">[Geist: Balance of Shadows</a><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/17537-v20-the-conspiracy-of-hrad-%C4%8Dern%C3%A1-hora-actual-play\" target=\"_blank\"> ][ Vampire: The Conspiracy of Hrad \u010cern\u00e1 Hora</a><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/13838-scion-bohemian-front-actual-play\" target=\"_blank\"> ][ Scion: Bohemian Front</a>][<a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/99033-changeling-malibu-dream-house-actual-play\" target=\"_blank\">Changeling: Malibu Dream House</a>] [<a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/demon-the-descent/268069-demon-night-train-detective-agency-actual-play\" target=\"_blank\">Demon: Night Train Detective Agency</a>] <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/la94fvk\" target=\"_blank\">[WoD: The Golden Eagle</a>]","edited":{"userid":56,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/56-baroness-nerak","user":"Baroness Nerak","date":". 12-23-2013, 07:48 PM"},"attachment